A||||8||86SE||Spoke to soon!! - - NO SPARK||||8||
Z||000000||86SE||03-21-2000||09:35 PM||||My car would not start, so I went out and bought a ignition coil and put it in.  It was about 8 PM when I started it up.  It started, and the tach bounced around for about 4 seconds when the engine was stumbling.  Then it cleared right up and ran fine!  I dident drive it, I just shut it off.  After school today I went out to start it, and it started right up, I restarted it a few times to make sure it will start when I want it to.  The last time I reved it up a little (3000 RPM) then it started missing really bad, then it stalled, and now it wont start (no spark).  When I put the new ignition coil in I took off the distrubutor and made sure everything in there was intact and it was all good (i think)??<P>What now?? Buy ANOTHER coil??  Or is the problem the the rotor thing inside the distributor that spins around??<P>THANKS!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||03-21-2000||09:46 PM||||This is a v-6 right.<BR>It sounds to me like the ignition module is bad. It sits just under the distributor cap, and has two set of wires plugged into one end. Take it out, and have it tested. Sounds very likely that is the culprit.<P>I have found that bumping those things when they are going out, tends to make them work intermittantly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||03-21-2000||09:47 PM||||Yeah, its a V-6.  I'll take it out and have it tested.  Thanks!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-21-2000||10:01 PM||||Just to let you know, the only place I found that can test electrical stuff out of the car is autozone, they do it all. Pepboys just gives this blank stare when you ask about anything electrical.||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||03-21-2000||10:07 PM||||Otherwise i'll put my hands over the distributor cap and have my friend turn it over!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000005||Oreif||03-22-2000||01:09 AM||||If you have no spark but you can smell fuel the ignition module is working. I bet your problem is the pick-up coil. Mine went out last year. You need to pull out the distributor and dis-assemble it to replace it. Go to Northern ILL Fiero Enthusiasts site<BR>at: <A HREF="http://www.xnet.com/%7Enife/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.xnet.com/%7Enife/</A>  and look under the tech area. It gives a detailed explanation on why and how to repair it!||||reg||1
Z||000006||wwh0001||03-22-2000||09:54 AM||||I agree it sounds like the pick up coil. actually when you take off the distributor cap you can see if it has "melted". Unfortunately you need to pull the distributor to replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||03-22-2000||09:43 PM||||I took a look at the pickup coil and its not melted but the fine copper wires that go around it a really brittle.||||reg||1
Z||000008||86SE||03-23-2000||11:15 PM||||ITS ALIVE!!!  I replaced the igniton coil and the ignition control module, turned it over and immediately it fired!  Only cost me $22 for a new module and $35 for the coil.||||reg||10
